WEBVTT

00:02.550 --> 00:07.260
Right now, dozens of spacecraft are observing the space around Earth and the Sun.

00:07.260 --> 00:10.730
Scientists call this fleet the Heliophysics System Observatory.

00:12.200 --> 00:19.790
The Sun constantly emits a wind of charged particles into space, and that wind carries the Sun's
magnetic field with it.

00:19.790 --> 00:25.960
Where it meets Earth's magnetic field, the two can snap and reconnect, releasing bursts of
energy.

00:25.960 --> 00:28.030
That's called magnetic reconnection.

00:28.030 --> 00:34.380
It powers the northern lights, and it drives space weather, which can disrupt satellites, GPS and
power grids.

00:35.850 --> 00:43.540
I'm Dr. Jason Beedle, and I study reconnection with NASA's Magnetospheric Multiscale mission,
or MMS.

00:43.540 --> 00:52.860
It flies four spacecraft in a tight pyramid formation to use the near-Earth environment as a
natural laboratory to study the physics behind reconnection.

00:53.980 --> 00:56.930
MMS is just one part of NASA's fleet.

00:56.930 --> 01:07.098
THEMIS, Cluster, and the Van Allen Probes have explored the complex plasma environments of
the near-Earth system, Wind and ACE act as upstream monitors, observing the solar wind

01:07.098 --> 01:12.900
before it reaches us, and the Parker Solar Probe has sampled the edges of the Sun's outer
atmosphere.

01:14.360 --> 01:16.240
All of this data is public.

01:16.240 --> 01:21.870
But using it means working across fifteen-plus websites, archives and software packages.

01:21.870 --> 01:25.740
Putting even one event in context takes significant effort and skill.

01:27.230 --> 01:33.270
So I wondered how hard it would be to bring all of this together, in one simple visual
environment.

01:33.270 --> 01:37.520
That became Helios: a virtual observatory for the space around Earth.

01:38.910 --> 01:45.140
Pick any moment, and Helios shows you where every spacecraft was, from low Earth orbit out to
Jupiter.

01:45.140 --> 01:47.540
Click on one to see what it measured.

01:47.540 --> 01:53.440
Jump to October 16th, 2015, when MMS flew right through the heart of a reconnection event.

01:53.440 --> 02:01.610
From there, you can slice up the particle data, compare all four spacecraft, or write your own
equations, without writing any code.

02:01.610 --> 02:07.160
You can watch the northern lights through cameras on the ground, next to the spacecraft
passing overhead.

02:07.160 --> 02:11.970
And you can save it all in a workspace that reopens just as you left it, even offline.

02:13.460 --> 02:14.830
It's fast, too.

02:14.830 --> 02:20.620
Some calculations run hundreds of times faster than the field-standard Python tools.

02:20.620 --> 02:24.670
And for every plot, you can see the code and the exact data files behind it.

02:25.980 --> 02:31.520
All of its data comes from public archives run by NASA, NOAA, ESA and others.

02:31.520 --> 02:35.590
Helios brings it together, so more people can explore the space around Earth.
